# Hold libiio at the version this firmware was built and measured against.
#
# WHY THIS ONE PACKAGE. Almost nothing Debian ships can change what the radio
# does - the kernel is ADI's 6.12 on the FAT partition and apt has no concept of
# it, and the drivers are in that kernel. libiio is the exception: iiod is the
# daemon every host tool on the other end of the USB link talks to, and libiio0
# is what it and the local tools link against.
#
# The high-speed transmit path in particular is an ABI contract between this
# libiio and the kernel's IIO buffer implementation - libiio probes
# BLOCK_FREE_IOCTL to decide whether cyclic mode is available, and falls back to
# read()/write() SILENTLY when it is not. A libiio that changed that behaviour
# would break `./devkit gpio-check`, the self-test's loopback tone and the MCP's
# transmit tools, and it would do it without an error message - which is the
# expensive kind of breakage, not the loud kind.
#
# So the version is pinned rather than left to `apt upgrade`. Moving it should
# be a deliberate act followed by re-running the self-test, not a side effect of
# routine maintenance.
#
# `src:libiio` covers every binary the source package produces - today iiod,
# libiio0 and libiio-utils - so a new one cannot slip in unpinned.
#
# Pin-Priority 1001 (above 1000) makes this version the only candidate, which is
# what blocks an upgrade. It also permits a downgrade back to it, deliberately:
# if a version ever does get installed some other way, `apt install libiio0`
# returns the board to a known state rather than leaving it ahead.
#
# TO MOVE IT: edit the version here, rebuild the rootfs (./build.sh), and re-run
#   ./devkit selftest --ssh   and   ./devkit gpio-check
# before trusting the result. Editing this file on a running board is fine for a
# test, but the card and the repo then disagree - see write-card.sh's guard.
#
# TRADE-OFF, stated plainly: this also blocks security updates for libiio. That
# is the intended behaviour on a board whose network is a USB cable to one PC,
# and it is the wrong default for anything internet-facing.

Package: src:libiio
Pin: version 0.26-2
Pin-Priority: 1001
